      "captured_at": null,
      "author": null,
      "contributor": null
    },
    {
      "id": "docs_userguide_pricing_tarf_tarf",
      "label": "Target Redemption Forward (TaRF)",
      "file_type": "concept",
      "source_file": "Docs/UserGuide/pricing/tarf.tex",
      "source_location": null,
      "source_url": null,
      "captured_at": null,
      "author": null,
      "contributor": null,
      "rationale": "An EQ/FX/Comm TaRF is priced using a Black-Scholes process for the underlying calibrated to its quoted market ATM volatility, valued by Monte Carlo simulation, because the target-redemption/accumulation feature requires path-dependent simulation; classified as SingleAssetOption."
    },
    {
      "id": "docs_userguide_pricing_totalreturnswap_total_return_swap",
      "label": "Generic Total Return Swap / Contract for Difference (CFD)",
      "file_type": "concept",
      "source_file": "Docs/UserGuide/pricing/totalReturnSwap.tex",
      "source_location": "Rationale for the accrual method; Limitation of the accrual method",
      "source_url": null,
      "captured_at": null,
      "author": null,
      "contributor": null,
      "rationale": "Priced using the accrual method (return leg NPV = change in underlying price plus paid cashflows since the last reset, converted via FX where needed; funding leg NPV = accrued interest of the current coupon period) rather than a full discounting valuation, because a TRS typically carries a daily unwind right and, even ignoring that right, the accrual method is shown algebraically to be equivalent to no-arbitrage valuation for the current return period under a deterministic short-rate assumption. Key limitations: it assumes the TRS can be closed out immediately at no cost (no make-whole fees, no hedge/repo/cross-currency unwind costs), whereas real early termination is expensive and rarely exercised; it assumes no gap or wrong-way risk even though TRS are commonly heavily overcollateralized (30%+ haircuts) precisely because wrong-way risk is material, so the model ignores meaningful counterparty risk; it assumes a theoretical repo discounting curve consistent with par funding, whereas real TRS are collateralized under ISDA at RFR/OIS rates that differ from asset-specific repo curves; it assumes continuous dividends (no discrete dividend dates) for equity underlyings, no ex-coupon dates for bonds, no early termination triggers besides bond default, non-callable underlyings for fixed-income TRS, and unrestricted settlement (no capital-control modelling for emerging-market TRS)."
    },
    {
      "id": "docs_userguide_pricing_var_and_vol_derivatives_exotic_variance_volatility_derivatives",
      "label": "Exotic Variance and Volatility Derivatives",
      "file_type": "concept",
      "source_file": "Docs/UserGuide/pricing/var_and_vol_derivatives.tex",
      "source_location": null,
      "source_url": null,
      "captured_at": null,
      "author": null,
      "contributor": null,
      "rationale": "Exotic variance/volatility swaps and options (including pairwise and basket variants) are priced using a Black-Scholes process for the underlying(s) calibrated to quoted market ATM volatility, valued via Monte Carlo simulation, because their exotic payoff structures cannot be captured by the closed-form replication formula used for vanilla variance swaps; classified as SingleAssetOption (or MultiAssetOption for pairwise/basket variants)."
    },
    {
      "id": "docs_userguide_pricing_window_barrieroption_window_barrier_option",
      "label": "Window Barrier Option",
      "file_type": "concept",
      "source_file": "Docs/UserGuide/pricing/window_barrieroption.tex",
      "source_location": null,
      "source_url": null,
      "captured_at": null,
      "author": null,
      "contributor": null,
      "rationale": "EQ/FX/COMM window barrier options are priced using a Black-Scholes process for the underlying calibrated to the option strike (rather than ATM vol) and valued via Monte Carlo simulation, in order to capture the barrier-monitoring window feature; classified as SingleAssetOption."
    },
    {
      "id": "docs_userguide_pricing_worstofbasketswap_worst_of_basket_swap",
      "label": "Worst Of Basket Swap",
      "file_type": "concept",
      "source_file": "Docs/UserGuide/pricing/worstofbasketswap.tex",
      "source_location": null,
      "source_url": null,
      "captured_at": null,
      "author": null,
      "contributor": null,
      "rationale": "Priced in a Hull White model calibrated to ATM coterminal swaptions, valued via Monte Carlo simulation, because the final equity amount depends on identifying the worst-performing asset in a basket at the final valuation date (with FX conversion into the payoff currency), requiring joint simulation of underlyings and IR curves; classified as MultiUnderlyingIrOption if the basket is all-InterestRate, otherwise IrHybrid."
    }
  ],
